Design an instant messaging application. It should support one-on-one chats and group chats, allowing users to send text messages, images, and files. Consider user authentication, message storage, and real-time capability in your design.

Example scale
{'users': 1000, 'messagesPerSecond': 500}

Implement a TradeProcessor class. It accepts incoming trades, lets clients subscribe to trades matching a filter with a callback, returns a handle for the subscription, and supports unsubscription. This is a coding-flavored design round where the prompt is deliberately under-specified — clarification is half the work.

Reported interface:

using HANDLE = int;
struct Trade { string symbol; int size; string flag; };
class TradeProcessor {
void onNewTrade(const Trade& trade);
HANDLE subscribe(const Trade& tradeFilter, function<void(const Trade&)> callback);
void unsubscribe(HANDLE handle);
};

Clarify before coding: what does the filter match on (exact symbol? flag? prefix?), is onNewTrade single- or multi-threaded, are callbacks invoked synchronously inline or dispatched, and does unsubscribe guarantee no further callbacks fire after it returns.

Design a system to support the following functions: add user, delete user, query user information, and update user information. The system should handle concurrent requests and ensure data consistency. Provide an architectural diagram (described) and explain the function of each module.

Example request
